===Verb===
{{head|en|verb form}}

# {{infl of|en|can||spast}}
#: {{ux|en|Before I was blind, I '''could''' see very well.}}
#: {{ux|en|When I was young everybody '''could''' easily find a job in a matter of days.}}
#: {{ux|en|When I was your age, I '''could''' run 10mi in under an hour.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''could''' only find the street, but I '''could'''n't locate his house.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''could''' distantly [[make out]] figures dancing in the village square.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''could''' put it anywhere or, [[at least]], he was told so.}} 
# {{inflection of|en|can||cond}}
## {{non-gloss|Used as a past subjunctive (contrary to fact).}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1921|author=William Alexander Fraser|title=Red Meekins|chapter=|url=https://archive.org/details/redmeekins00frasiala/redmeekins00frasiala/page/32/mode/1up|page=32|ol=6637709M|lccn=21017192|oclc=7515820|publisher=George H. Doran Company|text="If I'd had a paddle I '''could''''ve spun by that dashed root," Meekins said, feeling some apology due for the mishap. "When I first climbed out of that fool creek I thought my arm had got twisted off, but I guess it ain't."}}
##: ''I think he '''could''' do it if he really wanted to.''
##: ''I wish I '''could''' fly!''
##: {{ux|en|I'd have thought you '''could''' have done the dishes, at least.}} 
## {{non-gloss|Used to politely ask for [[permission]] to do something}}.
##: {{ux|en|'''Could''' I borrow your coat?}}
## {{non-gloss|Used to politely ask for someone else to do something}}.
##: {{ux|en|'''Could''' you proofread this email?}}
##: {{ux|en|I wonder, you '''could'''n't loan me the money, '''could''' you?}}
## {{non-gloss|Used to show the [[possibility]] that something [[might]] happen}}.
##* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-06-29|volume=407|issue=8842|page=55
|title=Travels and travails
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/international/21580191-edward-snowdens-odyssey-leaves-america-nonplussed-and-its-allies-dismayed-russia-china
|passage=Even without hovering drones, a lurking assassin, a thumping score and a denouement, the real-life story of Edward Snowden, a rogue spy on the run, '''could''' be straight out of the cinema. But, as with Hollywood, the subplots and exotic locations may distract from the real message: America’s discomfort and its foes’ glee.}}
##: {{ux|en|We '''could''' rearrange the time if you like.}}
## {{non-gloss|Used to [[suggest]] something}}.
##: {{ux|en|You '''could''' try adding more salt to the soup.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|except|Tyneside}} {{past participle of|en|can}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1981|year_published=1993|title=English Auxiliaries: Structure and History|author=Anthony Warner|page=222|isbn=9780521103213|text=I haven't '''could''' sleep.}}

====Usage notes====
* Some speakers and writers consider it wrong to use ''could'' to refer to permission. Such people favor replacing it with ''[[might]]'', just as they favor replacing ''can'' with ''[[may]]'' when referring to permission.

